WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.160
It's virtually guaranteed that if you're a&nbsp;
superhero in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.680
your family is full of tragedy. Even with the&nbsp;
stiff competition from folks like Thor and Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:16.500
Parker, it's hard to point out a super-family&nbsp;
with more ongoing drama than the Wakandan royalty.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.160
In "Captain America: Civil&nbsp;
War," King T'Chaka dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.920
leaving his distraught son T'Challa seeking&nbsp;
vengeance. In "Black Panther," we find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.560
that young T'Chaka killed his exiled criminal&nbsp;
brother N'Jobu, which sends the latter's son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.600
N'Jadaka on a path that turns him into&nbsp;
Killmonger, the movie's main antagonist.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.080
“I found my daddy with panther claws in&nbsp;
his chest. You ain't the son of a king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.010
you the son of a murderer.”

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.140
Warning: We're about to get into major spoilers.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.160
“Tell me the spoilers,&nbsp;
child…and embrace your destiny!”

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.680
"Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" doubles down&nbsp;
on the tragedy trend. In addition to depicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.720
T'Challa's death, a necessary plot point to&nbsp;
reflect the real-world death of star Chadwick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.180
Boseman, Queen Ramonda also dies during the&nbsp;
events of the movie. After Queen Ramonda's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.160
it seems that T'Challa's younger sister Shuri&nbsp;
is the only remaining member of her family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.260
leading her down a path of violent revenge…from&nbsp;
which she veers away at the last minute.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:19.680
However, the movie's mid-credits scene actually&nbsp;
reveals a new member of the Wakandan royal family:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.440
We discover that T'Challa actually fathered&nbsp;
a son with Nakia. As the latter introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.320
Shuri to young Toussaint, he reveals that&nbsp;
his Wakandan name is, in fact T'Challa.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.940
With a name like that, it seems like Marvel&nbsp;
Studios is setting the child up to become a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.880
part of the MCU many years down the line. Still,&nbsp;
major Marvel fans might already have noticed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:48.180
the MCU origin of the character is quite different&nbsp;
from T'Challa's son in the pages of Marvel Comics.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:53.520
There, his full name is Azari T'Challa —&nbsp;
and he's the son of T'Challa and Storm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.860
a longtime member of the X-Men. This version of&nbsp;
the character is actually from an alternate future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.360
timeline from the mainline Marvel Comics&nbsp;
timeline, and he's a teenage mutant who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.860
inherited both his father's Black Panther powers&nbsp;
and his mother's power to control lightning.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.700
This, of course, is a significantly&nbsp;
different origin story from the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.500
"Wakanda Forever's" T'Challa Jr. gets.&nbsp;
For starters, he's still just a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.680
child when we meet him. And we have&nbsp;
no idea whether he's inherited any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.460
of the powers that were granted to his&nbsp;
father by Wakanda's heart shaped herb.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.200
Though messing with a characters' background in&nbsp;
live action adaptations might annoy some fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.080
it's understandable that the MCU plays fast and&nbsp;
loose with T'Challa's family tree. Since Storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.540
hasn't been officially introduced in the MCU, and&nbsp;
"Black Panther" star Chadwick Boseman's death in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.900
2020 ended King T'Challa's story, there's no way&nbsp;
for the two to meet — let alone start a family.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.000
Meanwhile, Nakia is T'Challa's former&nbsp;
lover and obviously ongoing romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.480
interest in "Black Panther," and&nbsp;
her lengthy absence from the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.200
after the first "Black Panther" movie&nbsp;
is easily explained by the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.040
she's been in Haiti, keeping the heir&nbsp;
to Wakanda's throne safe and hidden.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.697
It's an interesting move to introduce T'Challa's&nbsp;
son at this exact point in the MCU timeline.

00:03:07.697 --> 00:03:11.580
Not only does his existence strongly hint&nbsp;
that the Black Panther's legacy will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.740
continue even after Shuri — but it also works&nbsp;
wonders for her, the current Black Panther.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:22.260
After all, Shuri's lost all the family members&nbsp;
that she knew of in relatively rapid succession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.540
and is finally starting to resolve her grief&nbsp;
and trauma by the time "Wakanda Forever" ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.180
The mid-credit scene's revelation that she's not&nbsp;
the last living member of her family dovetails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.920
perfectly with her character arc of accepting&nbsp;
her losses, and honoring her family's legacy.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.980
"Black Panther: Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever" is now in theaters.

